hl g1fwxfi0 g1fwxfii\",\"data-contentful-field-id\":\"content\",\"data-contentful-entry-id\":\"19zVl3oMY5SLldffH0jykp\",\"id\":\"main-content-4\",\"data-sentry-element\":\"Component\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"box.tsx\",\"data-sentry-component\":\"Box\",\"children\":[null,[[\"$\",\"h2\",\"0\",{\"className\":\"_15il2ff2 _3unhtx1 g1fwxf99 g1fwxf9g _42elsx0 g1fwxfq3\",\"id\":\"It-was-traveling-too-fast.\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"b\",\"0\",{\"children\":\"It was traveling too fast.\"}]],\"data-sentry-element\":\"H\",\"data-sentry-component\":\"H2\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"heading.tsx\"}],[\"$\",\"div\",\"1\",{\"className\":\"_42elsxa _1g6q0dv3 g1fwxfrj\",\"data-sentry-element\":\"Component\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"box.tsx\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"From the beginning, some blamed the Titanic’s skipper, Captain E.J. Smith, for sailing the massive ship at such a high speed (22 knots) through the iceberg-heavy waters of the North Atlantic. Some believed Smith was trying to better the crossing time of Titanic’s White Star sister ship, the Olympic. But in a 2004 paper, engineer Robert Essenhigh \",[\"$\",\"$L18\",\"1\",{\"target\":\"_blank\",\"rel\":\"noopener noreferrer\",\"children\":[\"speculated\"],\"data-sentry-element\":\"NextLink\",\"data-sentry-component\":\"Link\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"link.tsx\",\"href\":\"https://www.sciencedaily.com/releases/2004/11/041108020906.htm\",\"className\":\"_1l8442v0 _1jnz0ocj g1fwxfhu _1l8442v1 g1fwxfhi\",\"prefetch\":false}],\" that efforts to control a fire in one of the ship’s coal bunkers could have explained why the Titanic was sailing at full speed.\"]}],\"data-sentry-component\":\"Box\"}],[\"$\",\"h2\",\"2\",{\"className\":\"_15il2ff2 _3unhtx1 g1fwxf99 g1fwxf9g _42elsx0 g1fwxfq3\",\"id\":\"The-wireless-radio-operator-dismissed-a-key-iceberg-warning.\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"b\",\"0\",{\"children\":\"The wireless radio operator dismissed a key iceberg warning.\"}]],\"data-sentry-element\":\"H\",\"data-sentry-component\":\"H2\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"heading.tsx\"}],[\"$\",\"div\",\"3\",{\"className\":\"_42elsxa _1g6q0dv3 g1fwxfrj\",\"data-sentry-element\":\"Component\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"box.tsx\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"The Titanic had received multiple radio reports of ice from other ships throughout the day. Less than an hour before it hit the iceberg, the ship Californian radioed to say it had been stopped by dense field ice. But as the warning didn’t begin with the prefix “MSG” (Master’s Service Gram), which would have required the captain to directly acknowledge receiving the message, the Titanic’s radio operator Jack Phillips considered the other ship’s warning non-urgent, and \",[\"$\",\"$L18\",\"1\",{\"target\":\"_blank\",\"rel\":\"noopener noreferrer\",\"children\":[\"didn’t pass it along\"],\"data-sentry-element\":\"NextLink\",\"data-sentry-component\":\"Link\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"link.tsx\",\"href\":\"http://www.nbcnews.com/id/47018360/ns/technology_and_science/t/wireless-could-have-saved-lives-titanic\",\"className\":\"_1l8442v0 _1jnz0ocj g1fwxfhu _1l8442v1 g1fwxfhi\",\"prefetch\":false}],\".\"]}],\"data-sentry-component\":\"Box\"}],[\"$\",\"h2\",\"4\",{\"className\":\"_15il2ff2 _3unhtx1 g1fwxf99 g1fwxf9g _42elsx0 g1fwxfq3\",\"id\":\"It-may-have-taken-a-fatal-wrong-turn.\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"b\",\"0\",{\"children\":\"It may have taken a fatal wrong turn.\"}]],\"data-sentry-element\":\"H\",\"data-sentry-component\":\"H2\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"heading.tsx\"}],[\"$\",\"div\",\"5\",{\"className\":\"_42elsxa _1g6q0dv3 g1fwxfrj\",\"data-sentry-element\":\"Component\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"box.tsx\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"According to a \",[\"$\",\"$L18\",\"1\",{\"target\":\"_blank\",\"rel\":\"noopener noreferrer\",\"children\":[\"claim made in 2010\"],\"data-sentry-element\":\"NextLink\",\"data-sentry-component\":\"Link\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"link.tsx\",\"href\":\"https://www.telegraph.co.uk/culture/books/booknews/8016751/The-truth-about-the-sinking-of-the-Titanic.html\",\"className\":\"_1l8442v0 _1jnz0ocj g1fwxfhu _1l8442v1 g1fwxfhi\",\"prefetch\":false}],\" by Louise Patten (the granddaughter of the most senior Titanic officer to survive, Charles Lightoller), one of the ship’s crewmembers panicked after hearing the order to turn “hard-a-starboard” in order to avoid the approaching iceberg. Because ships at the time operated on two different steering order systems, he became confused and turned the wrong way—directly toward the ice. Patten included this version of events, which she said she heard from her grandmother after Lightoller’s death, in her fictionalized account of the Titanic disaster, \",[\"$\",\"i\",\"3\",{\"children\":\"Good as Gold\"}],\".\"]}],\"data-sentry-component\":\"Box\"}]]]}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"x4or4w5 x4or4w6 g1fwxfk g1fwxfs\",\"data-sentry-element\":\"Component\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"box.tsx\",\"data-sentry-component\":\"Box\",\"children\":[null,[\"$\",\"$L2e\",null,{\"mainContainerId\":\"main-content-4\",\"relatedArticles\":[{\"sys\":{\"id\":\"4NzGpy6fhTW0rPmNC5Us8a\",\"createdAt\":\"2025-01-21T14:26:11.511Z\"},\"componentType\":\"featureArticle\",\"title\":\"Titanic’s Tennis Star Survivors\",\"homepageHeadline\":null,\"slug\":\"titanics-tennis-star-survivors\",\"heroImage\":{\"fields\":{\"asset\":[{\"asset_id\":\"2372757146ce65a635c832303d528901\",\"public_id\":\"sinking-of-the-titanic-gettyimages-542907919-1\",\"version\":1737376078,\"version_id\":\"1e86ecd67f21f7464cce5b6809f159cf\",\"signature\":\"aabdc68b077d1d5f8dfcf75ca18f5f990ce05621\",\"width\":1920,\"height\":1079,\"format\":\"jpg\",\"resource_type\":\"image\",\"created_at\":\"2025-01-20T12:27:58Z\",\"tags\":[],\"pages\":1,\"bytes\":279275,\"type\":\"upload\",\"etag\":\"9a6bc2a2761b4d9e4dc66503e48f949b\",\"placeholder\":false,\"url\":\"http://res.cloudinary.com/aenetworks/image/upl