tegories\",\"articleName\":\"why-did-the-titanic-sink\",\"topic\":\"/early20thcenturyus\",\"quickFacts\":\"$undefined\",\"quickFactsLogo\":\"$6:2:props:children:5:0:props:children:1:1:props:children:1:props:quickFactsLogo\",\"data-sentry-element\":\"RightRail\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"rich-text-section.tsx\"}]]}]]]}],[\"$\",\"$1\",\"full-bleed-5\",{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"div\",\"0\",{\"className\":\"x4or4w6 i94vss1 i94vss4 g1fwxf30 g1fwxf4l g1fwxfiu g1fwxfho g1fwxfrf g1fwxfeu\",\"data-name\":\"section\",\"data-sentry-element\":\"Component\",\"data-sentry-component\":\"Box\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"box.tsx\",\"children\":[null,[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"g1fwxf2 g1fwxf14\",\"data-sentry-element\":\"Component\",\"data-sentry-component\":\"Box\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"box.tsx\",\"children\":[null,[[\"$\",\"$L2f\",null,{\"name\":\"The Titanic's Structure\",\"description\":\"Naval architect Roger Long breaks down Titanic's structure to gain better insight into how and why the \\\"unsinkable\\\" ship sank.\",\"thumbnailUrl\":\"https://public-content-aetn.video.aetnd.com/video-thumbnails/AETN-History_VMS/21/165/History_Titanic_The_Titanics_Structure_reSF_HD.jpg\",\"duration\":\"181\",\"currentUrl\":\"https://www.history.com\",\"createdAt\":\"2012-05-30T16:10:07.000Z\"}],\"$L32\"]]}]]}]]}],[\"$\",\"div\",\"main-content-6\",{\"className\":\"i94vss1 i94vss4 g1fwxfiu g1fwxfho g1fwxfrf g1fwxfeu\",\"data-name\":\"section\",\"data-sentry-element\":\"Component\",\"data-sentry-component\":\"Box\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"box.tsx\",\"children\":[null,[[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"g1fwxf4 g1fwxf7 g1fwxfy g1fwxfqz g1fwxfhl g1fwxfi0 g1fwxfii\",\"data-contentful-field-id\":\"content\",\"data-contentful-entry-id\":\"19zVl3oMY5SLldffH0jykp\",\"id\":\"main-content-6\",\"data-sentry-element\":\"Component\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"box.tsx\",\"data-sentry-component\":\"Box\",\"children\":[null,[[\"$\",\"h2\",\"0\",{\"className\":\"_15il2ff2 _3unhtx1 g1fwxf99 g1fwxf9g _42elsx0 g1fwxfq3\",\"id\":\"Some-of-the-rivets-that-held-the-ship-together-may-have-been-weak.\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"b\",\"0\",{\"children\":\"Some of the rivets that held the ship together may have been weak.\"}]],\"data-sentry-element\":\"H\",\"data-sentry-component\":\"H2\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"heading.tsx\"}],[\"$\",\"div\",\"1\",{\"className\":\"_42elsxa _1g6q0dv3 g1fwxfrj\",\"data-sentry-element\":\"Component\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"box.tsx\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"In 1985, when an American-French expedition finally located the historic wreck, investigators discovered that, contrary to earlier findings, the Titanic had not sunk intact after hitting the iceberg but had broken apart on the ocean’s surface. Materials scientists Tim Foecke and Jennifer Hooper McCarty \",[\"$\",\"$L18\",\"1\",{\"target\":\"_blank\",\"rel\":\"noopener noreferrer\",\"children\":[\"have cast blame\"],\"data-sentry-element\":\"NextLink\",\"data-sentry-component\":\"Link\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"link.tsx\",\"href\":\"https://www.usnews.com/news/national/articles/2008/09/25/the-secret-of-how-the-titanic-sunk\",\"className\":\"_1l8442v0 _1jnz0ocj g1fwxfhu _1l8442v1 g1fwxfhi\",\"prefetch\":false}],\" on some of the more than 3 million rivets that held the hull’s steel plates together. They examined rivets brought up from the wreck and found them to contain a high concentration of “slag,” a smelting residue that can make metal split apart. This may have weakened the part of the Titanic’s hull that hit the iceberg, causing it to break apart upon impact.\"]}],\"data-sentry-component\":\"Box\"}],[\"$\",\"h2\",\"2\",{\"className\":\"_15il2ff2 _3unhtx1 g1fwxf99 g1fwxf9g _42elsx0 g1fwxfq3\",\"id\":\"Mirages-and-hazy-horizons-were-created-by-weather-conditions.\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"b\",\"0\",{\"children\":\"Mirages and hazy horizons were created by weather conditions.\"}]],\"data-sentry-element\":\"H\",\"data-sentry-component\":\"H2\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"heading.tsx\"}],[\"$\",\"div\",\"3\",{\"className\":\"_42elsxa _1g6q0dv3 g1fwxfrj\",\"data-sentry-element\":\"Component\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"box.tsx\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"Two studies done around the time of the 100th anniversary of the Titanic disaster in 2012 suggested that nature played a key role in the ship’s fate. The first argued that the Earth came unusually close to both the moon and the sun that year, increasing their gravitational pull on the ocean and producing record tides, which caused increased amounts of floating ice in the North Atlantic around the time of the sinking.\"]}],\"data-sentry-component\":\"Box\"}],[\"$\",\"div\",\"4\",{\"className\":\"_42elsxa _1g6q0dv3 g1fwxfrj\",\"data-sentry-element\":\"Component\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"box.tsx\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"The second study, by British historian Tim Maltin, claimed that atmospheric conditions on the night of the disaster might have caused a phenomenon called \",[\"$\",\"$L18\",\"1\",{\"target\":\"_blank\",\"rel\":\"noopener noreferrer\",\"children\":[\"super refraction\"],\"data-sentry-element\":\"NextLink\",\"data-sentry-component\":\"Link\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"link.tsx\",\"href\":\"https://www.smithsonianmag.com/science-nature/did-the-titanic-sink-because-of-an-optical-illusion-102040309/\",\"className\":\"_1l8442v0 _1jnz0ocj g1fwxfhu _1l8442v1 g1fwxfhi\",\"prefetch\":false}],\". This bending of light could have created mirages, or optical illusions, that prevented the Titanic’s lookouts from seeing the iceberg clearly. It also would have made the Titanic appear closer, and smaller, to the nearby ship the Californian, causing its crew to assume it was a different ship without a radio, preventing them from attempting to communicate. From their vantage point, and with these hazy conditions, when the Titanic started to sink, the Californian’s crew would have thought